// (1) EVENT LOOP: what runs first? Microtasks (promises) before macerotasks.
console.log(1);
setTimeout(() => console.log(2), 0);        // macrotask
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3)); // microtask
console.log(4);
// Output: 1, 4, 3, 2  (sync code first, then microtasks, then macrotasks)

// (2) CLOSURE: a function retains its lexical scope. `let` gives each
// iteration its own binding, so each callback sees its own i.
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);      // 0, 1, 2 (with `let`)
}
//   With `var`, all three would print 3 (one shared binding) — a classic
//   interview question that tests closures + the loop's per-iteration scope.

// (3) React hooks ARE closures: useState returns a value captured in the
//   render's scope. Stale closures (capturing an old value) are a common bug
//   the event-loop/closure questions screen for.

